  "question": {
    "text": "<p>Two cells of the same EMF&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>E</mi></math> but different internal resistances <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> are connected in series with an external resistance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math> as shown in the figure. The terminal potential difference across, the second cell is found to be zero. The external resistance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math> must then be:</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"assets\\qt0174_c058d4785ad6.jpeg\" style=\"width: 250px; height: 176px;\" /></p>"
  },
